    "slot_number": "slot-7-2-dyn-5",
    "slot_key": "Real-time dashboard fields for command/state monitoring",
    "slot_value": "Current commanded state, confirmed field state, any device that hasn’t responded, conflicts between requested lane position and field-reported state, time of the last successful update, active alarms, communication losses, and clear indication when data is stale, partial, degraded, or not yet confirmed current. The dashboard should time out old state, avoid blending stale data with live status, and make uncertainty visible immediately so operators know whether they are looking at verified field status before issuing open or close commands.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-3-1-dyn-6",
    "slot_key": "Delay-trigger conditions",
    "slot_value": "Delay the lane open or close sequence if system status is stale or incomplete, if any critical device is still reporting a transition or fault, if the operator request does not match the current operating schedule, if communications are unstable enough that command delivery/confirmation cannot be trusted, or if there is an active conflict such as another sequence already running or a safety interlock that has not cleared. Also delay or avoid confirming the state when a device briefly drops off, an update is partial, or the old state has timed out and can no longer be trusted.",
    "operation": "refine"
  },
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-3-1-dyn-8",
    "slot_key": "Communications uncertainty handling",
    "slot_value": "Any loss of communications to a critical element should be treated as safety-impacting if it prevents positive confirmation of lane status or prevents an essential control command from being sent. Uncertainty about a safety-critical state should be escalated immediately rather than left as a routine alarm. If confirmation is delayed, missing, or contradictory, operators should not treat the lane as safe to rely on automatically; the system should flag an alarm or uncertainty condition and require manual verification or fallback procedures before anyone acts on the result. Stale data should be very obvious and not blended in with live status; if the system cannot confirm the state, it should make that uncertainty visible right away rather than trying to guess.",
    "operation": "refine"
  },
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-3-1-3",
    "slot_key": "Post-command verification and confirmation steps",
    "slot_value": "After sending the command, operators should get fast confirmation that the system accepted it and visible feedback as devices move through the expected state; the system should clearly show success, pending, or failure so they are not left guessing. For occasional operators, it should show each step of the transition and a clear confirmation when the operation is complete, rather than leaving them to infer it from raw device data. Operators should see clear indication that the commanded lane position was actually achieved, with the relevant field devices reporting a matching open or closed state and the overall system showing the lane as confirmed in that position. Confirmed state should not be shown when the data is stale, partial, or no longer current; degraded status should be clearly marked instead.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-3-2-1",
    "slot_key": "Status indicators required during lane operations",
    "slot_value": "Field-device and overall system status should clearly show that the commanded lane position was actually achieved, not just that the command was sent; relevant field devices should report a matching state like open or closed and the system should show the lane as confirmed in that position. If confirmation is delayed, missing, or contradictory, operators should not treat the lane as safe to rely on automatically; the system should flag an alarm or uncertainty condition and require manual verification or fallback procedures before anyone acts on the result. Operators would also need to see the current commanded state, the confirmed field state, any device that hasn’t responded, any conflict between the requested lane position and what the field devices are reporting, the time of the last successful update, and any active alarms or communication losses. Stale data should be very obvious and not blended in with live status; if a device drops off briefly or an update is partial, the dashboard should clearly mark it as degraded, time out the old state, and avoid showing it as confirmed if it isn’t current. If the system can’t confirm the state, it should make that uncertainty visible right away rather than trying to guess.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

